### 1. Preliminaries  

- **Quote:** “Define \(T_n=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid a+b\le n+1\}\). … A simple count gives \(|T_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2\).”  
  **Verification:** The set \(T_n\) consists of all lattice points in the right‑angled triangle with vertices \((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\). For each \(a=1,\dots ,n\) there are \(n+1-a\) admissible \(b\)’s, so \(\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\frac{n(n+1)}2\). The count is correct.

- **Quote:** “For a column \(x=c\) (\(1\le c\le n\)) the points of \(T_n\) … contain \(h_c=n+1-c\) points. In particular \(h_n=1,\;h_{n-1}=2,\;h_{n-2}=3\).”  
  **Verification:** Directly follows from the definition of \(T_n\). Correct.

---

### 2. Points on a sunny line  

- **Quote:** “Let \(L\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \(T_n\). Write its reduced slope as \(m=p/q\)… the values of \(s=x+y\) taken by integer points of \(L\) form an arithmetic progression whose common difference is \(|p+q|\).”  
  **Verification:** If \((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\in L\cap\mathbb Z^2\) then \((x_2-x_1)=qt,\;(y_2-y_1)=pt\). Hence \(s_2-s_1=(p+q)t\). Since \((p,q)=1\) and \(q>0\), the step size in the sum \(x+y\) is \(|p+q|\). The argument is rigorous.

- **Quote:** “Thus the number of points of \(T_n\) that can lie on \(L\) is at most \(\bigl\lfloor\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1\le\bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1\). Because \(|p+q|\ge1\)… a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point.”  
  **Verification:** The bound follows from the range \(2\le s\le n+1\). Since \(|p+q|\ge1\) (equality would give \(p=-q\), excluded), the bound is valid. The observation that two points with the same \(x\) would force a vertical line (non‑sunny) is correct. Hence a sunny line meets any fixed column in at most one point.

All statements in this section are fully justified.

