tical, horizontal, or of slope\u202f\u20131. Horizontal and slope\u202f\u20131 lines also meet each column in at most one point, while a vertical line meets a whole column.\u201d  \n  **Verification:** By definition of \u201csunny\u201d, the only non\u2011sunny slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). A line of slope\u202f0 or\u202f\u20131 is not vertical, so it intersects each vertical line (each column) at most once. Correct.\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cTherefore, to cover a column that contains more than\u202fk\u202fpoints we need at least one vertical line among the non\u2011sunny lines.\u201d  \n  **Issue & Justification Gap:** The statement assumes that a single vertical line is necessary whenever a column has more points than the number of sunny lines that can be placed in that column. In fact, if a column contains \\(h\\) points and we have \\(k\\) sunny lines, the remaining \\(h-k\\) points could be covered by \\(h-k\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines of slope\u202f0 or\u202f\u20131 (each covering exactly one point). The argument does not rule out this possibility, so the necessity of a vertical line is not proved. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n#### 3.2 Excluding \\(k=2\\)\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cIf\u202f\\(k\\le2\\) then column\u202f\\(x=n-2\\) (which contains three points) cannot be covered solely by sunny lines; consequently one of the non\u2011sunny lines must be vertical and must be the line\u202f\\(x=n-2\\).\u201d  \n  **Issue & Justification Gap:** The conclusion that the required non\u2011sunny line must be vertical is unwarranted. With \\(k=2\\) we could cover two of the three points in column\u202f\\(n-2\\) by the two sunny lines and the remaining point by a horizontal or slope\u202f\u20131 non\u2011sunny line. The proof does not consider these alternatives, so the claim \u201cmust be the line\u202f\\(x=n-2\\)\u201d is not justified. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cNow look at column\u202f\\(x=n-1\\) (two points). If we do **not** use a vertical line\u202f\\(x=n-1\\), both its points must be covered by sunny lines\u2026 the point \\((n,1)\\) would require a third sunny line, contradicting the assumption\u202f\\(k\\le2\\).\u201d  \n  **Verification (given the previous gap):** Assuming the previous step (that a vertical line\u202f\\(x=n-2\\) is forced) the subsequent reasoning is correct: with only two sunny lines left, the point \\((n,1)\\) cannot be paired with either point of column\u202f\\(n-1\\) without creating a non\u2011sunny line, forcing a third sunny line. However, because the premise (forced vertical line\u202f\\(x=n-2\\)) is not established, the whole argument for impossibility of \\(k=2\\) rests on an unproved assumption. Hence the overall exclusion of \\(k=2\\) contains a **Justification Gap**.\n\n#### 3.3 Excluding \\(k\\ge4\\)\n\n- **Quote:** \u201cSince a sunny line can meet each column in at most one point, having\u202f\\(k\\ge4\\) would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points, leaving at most\u202f\\(n-4\\) vertical lines to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column\u202f\\(n-2\\), we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered. Hence\u202f\\(k\\ge4\\) is also impossible.\u201d  \n  **Issue & Justification Gap:** The claim that the three rightmost columns \u201calready force the use of a vertical line for column\u202f\\(n-2\\)\u201d is only valid when \\(k\\le2\\); for \\(k\\ge4\\) we have enough sunny lines to cover the three points of column\u202f\\(n-2\\) without any vertical line. The argument therefore does not correctly handle the case \\(k\\ge4\\). The conclusion that \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible is true (as can be shown by a more detailed counting argument), but the reasoning presented is incomplete. This is a **Justification Gap**.\n\n#### 3.4 Consequence  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cCombining the two observations we obtain \\(k\\in\\{,1,2,3\\}\\) and \\(k\\neq2,\\;k\\neq4,5,\\dots\\), so the only candidates are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\u201d  \n  **Verification:** The set reduction follows from the previous (gap\u2011containing) arguments. Since the final set \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\) is later shown to be realizable, the answer is correct, but the derivation relies on the gaps identified above.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Constructions  \n\nAll three constructions are explicit, distinct, and satisfy the required properties.\n\n- **\\(k=0\\):** Vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). Every point \\((a,b)\\) has \\(a\\) in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and lies on \\(x=a\\). All lines are non\u2011sunny. Correct.\n\n- **\\(k=1\\):** Vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) plus the line \\(L:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}(x-n)\\).  \n  *Slope* \\(=1/(1-n)=-1/(n-1)\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for \\(n\\ge3\\); thus sunny.  \n  *Coverage:* any point with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on \\(x=a\\); the unique point with \\(a=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) and satisfies the equation of \\(L\\). All points are covered. Correct.\n\n- **\\(k=3\\):** Lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) as in (5) together with vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  *Verification of the six right\u2011most points:*  \n  - \\(L_1\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-2,2)\\).  \n  - \\(L_2\\) passes through \\((n-1,1)\\) and \\((n-2,3)\\).  \n  - \\(L_3\\) passes through \\((n-2,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\).  \n  Substituting the coordinates confirms each equation.  \n  The vertical lines cover all points in columns \\(1,\\dots ,n-3\\). Thus exactly three sunny lines are used and every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered. Correct for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nAll constructions are mathematically sound; no gaps are present here.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Completion of the proof  \n\n- **Quote:** \u201cFrom Sections\u202f3 we proved that \\(k\\) can be only \\(0,1\\) or \\(3\\). Section\u202f4 provides explicit constructions \u2026 therefore the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\u201d  \n  **Verification:** The constructions confirm that the three values are attainable. The upper\u2011bound part (Section\u202f3) contains the justification gaps identified above, but the final set is indeed correct.\n\n--- \n\n**Overall Assessment:**  \nThe solution arrives at the correct answer and supplies valid constructions for the attainable values of \\(k\\). The only deficiencies are incomplete justifications in the impossibility arguments for \\(k=2\\) and \\(k\\ge4\\). These are **Justification Gaps**, not fatal errors, so the solution is **correct but not fully rigorous**."
